From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: h713: point the IOMMU at the real block and attach the VE

The iommu node has been sitting at 0x030f0000 -- the H6's base, where this
SoC has nothing. Every register there reads 0, including ENABLE and TTB,
which an attached domain would have written. That is why the ve node's
iommus property was removed on 2026-08-09: pointing cedrus at an inert
IOMMU is worse than having none, because the DMA layer hands out IOVAs
believing something will translate them, and the VE writes them as physical
addresses. It corrupted the printk ringbuffer on the first decode.

The stock DTB on board B's own eMMC says where the block actually is, and
the block confirms it. With the clock gated on -- CCU 0x7bc bit 0, read
first because probing an ungated block hangs this interconnect -- writing
0xdeadb000 to TTB reads back 0xdead8000, so it answers and wants the same
16 KiB-aligned directory table as the H6. RESET reads 0x8003007f and BYPASS
0x0000007f: seven masters, 0 through 6, all bypassing at reset.

So the node moves to 0x2010000 with the stock interrupt (SPI 71) and clock,
gains the two-cell binding, and drops the resets property, which the stock
node does not have and our CCU does not provide.

The ve node gets `iommus = <&iommu 0 1>` -- master 0, translation on,
exactly what the stock tree does. Beyond correctness, this is containment:
untranslated, cedrus writing after its DMA buffers are freed reaches
arbitrary kernel memory instead of raising a fault, which is the bug patch
0040 addresses and the reason six unrelated kernel structures were corrupted
while chasing it.

The display and ge2d are left alone. The stock tree has them on master 2
with translation OFF, and there is no reason to differ before the video
decode path is proven with the IOMMU live.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1103,14 +1103,30 @@
 		 * dma-direct on CMA instead of being handed IOVAs that nothing
 		 * translates.
 		 */
-		iommu: iommu@30f0000 {
-			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h6-iommu";
-			reg = <0x030f0000 0x10000>;
-			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 24 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		/*
+		 * Every value here is read out of the stock DTB on board B's
+		 * own eMMC (`local/h713-lab/captures/board-b/`), not inferred
+		 * from the H6. The previous node lived at the H6's 0x030f0000,
+		 * where this SoC has nothing: every register read 0, including
+		 * ENABLE and TTB, which an attached domain would have written.
+		 *
+		 * Confirmed on hardware 2026-08-17 after gating the clock on
+		 * (the block is clocked off at reset, and probing it ungated
+		 * hangs this interconnect, so read CCU 0x7bc bit 0 first):
+		 * writing 0xdeadb000 to TTB reads back 0xdead8000, so it
+		 * answers and wants a 16 KiB-aligned table exactly as the H6
+		 * block does. RESET reads 0x8003007f and BYPASS 0x0000007f --
+		 * seven masters, 0-6, all bypassing translation at reset.
+		 *
+		 * No resets property: the stock node has only a clock, and
+		 * there is no IOMMU reset line in our CCU.
+		 */
+		iommu: iommu@2010000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-iommu";
+			reg = <0x02010000 0x1000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 71 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
 			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_IOMMU>;
-			resets = <&ccu 11>;
-			#iommu-cells = <1>;
-			status = "disabled";
+			#iommu-cells = <2>;
 		};
 
 		/*
@@ -1196,27 +1212,31 @@
 			 */
 			power-domains = <&ppu 3>;
 			/*
-			 * NO iommus PROPERTY -- deliberate, 2026-08-09.
-			 *
-			 * `iommus = <&iommu 0>` here crashed the kernel on the
-			 * first decode: rogue VE DMA corrupted the printk
-			 * ringbuffer, faulting in _prb_read_valid() with a
-			 * recursive fault storm and a stack-overflow panic,
-			 * before a single frame was emitted (0-byte output).
-			 *
-			 * Cause: the iommu node below is inert. Every register
-			 * at 0x030f0000 reads 0 on hardware, including ENABLE
-			 * (+0x20) and TTB (+0x30), which an attached domain
-			 * would have written. So the DMA layer handed cedrus
-			 * IOVAs expecting translation, nothing translated them,
-			 * and the VE wrote them as raw physical addresses.
+			 * Master 0, translation on -- exactly what the stock
+			 * DTB does (`iommus = <&mmu_aw 0 1>`).
 			 *
-			 * Without the property cedrus uses dma-direct on CMA,
-			 * where the addresses it programs are real allocations.
-			 * Restore this only once the IOMMU is verified live --
-			 * an inert IOMMU is worse than none, because it removes
-			 * the bounds the DMA layer believes are being enforced.
+			 * This property was deliberately absent from
+			 * 2026-08-09 until 2026-08-17, and the reason it is
+			 * back is worth keeping. It was removed because
+			 * `iommus = <&iommu 0>` crashed the kernel on the first
+			 * decode: rogue VE DMA corrupted the printk ringbuffer
+			 * and panicked in _prb_read_valid() before a frame came
+			 * out. The cause was not the property but the node it
+			 * pointed at -- the H6's 0x030f0000, where this SoC has
+			 * nothing, so the DMA layer handed cedrus IOVAs that
+			 * nothing translated and the VE wrote them as physical
+			 * addresses. An inert IOMMU is worse than none.
+			 *
+			 * The node now points at the real block, which has been
+			 * verified to answer on hardware (see the iommu node),
+			 * so the bounds the DMA layer believes in are actually
+			 * enforced. That matters beyond correctness here:
+			 * without translation, cedrus writing after its buffers
+			 * are freed corrupts arbitrary kernel memory rather
+			 * than raising a fault -- see
+			 * docs/vaapi-scope.md and patch 0040.
 			 */
+			iommus = <&iommu 0 1>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
